In $\triangle ADK$ and $\triangle BDC$:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{AK}{AD} &= \frac{BC}{BD} \\
AK \times BD &= AD \times BC \quad \text{(1)}
\end{align*}$$
In $\triangle ADB$ and $\triangle KDC$:
$$\begin{align*}
\frac{KC}{CD} &= \frac{AB}{BD} \\
KC \times BD &= CD \times AB \quad \text{(2)}
\end{align*}$$
Adding (1) and (2):
$$\begin{align*}
AK \times BD + KC \times BD &= AD \times BC + CD \times AB \\
BD(AK + KC) &= AD \times BC + AB \times DC \\
BD \times AC &= AD \times BC + AB \times DC
\end{align*}$$

Applying the result from part (ii) to the cyclic quadrilateral formed by four consecutive vertices of the pentagon. The side lengths are $1, 1, 1$ and the base chord is $x$. The diagonals are $x, x$:
$$\begin{align*}
x \times x &= 1 \times 1 + 1 \times x \\
x^2 &= x + 1 \\
x^2 - x - 1 &= 0 \\
x &= \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{1 - 4(1)(-1)}}{2} \\
x &= \frac{1 \pm \sqrt{5}}{2}
\end{align*}$$
Since $x > 0$:
$$\begin{align*}
x &= \frac{1 + \sqrt{5}}{2}
\end{align*}$$

